%!PS
% SPDX-FileCopyrightText: 2026 EfterScript contributors
% SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
% backend: none
% expect-output: 801D0D065101C4EA69319AC4062391BCEE6E10198DE78808>
% expect-output: 801D0D065101C4EA69319AC4062391BCEE6E10198DE78808>
% expect-output: the quick brown fox
% expect-output: true
% Scenario: LZWEncode writing through an ASCIIHexEncode filter to
% standard output. No two-byte sequence of `the quick brown fox`
% repeats, so the stream is the clear code 256, the nineteen bytes as
% their own codes, and the end code 257: twenty-one nine-bit codes,
% packed most-significant bit first into 24 bytes, the last three bits
% zero. Twenty-one codes never widen the code, so EarlyChange 0 writes
% the same bytes. The text read back through LZWDecode gives the bytes,
% and the EarlyChange 0 stream decodes with EarlyChange 0.
% Expect: the hexadecimal text twice, the decoded bytes, true.
(%stdout) (w) file /ASCIIHexEncode filter
<< /CloseTarget true >> /LZWEncode filter
dup (the quick brown fox) writestring closefile
(\n) print
(%stdout) (w) file /ASCIIHexEncode filter
<< /CloseTarget true /EarlyChange 0 >> /LZWEncode filter
dup (the quick brown fox) writestring closefile
(\n) print
<801D0D065101C4EA69319AC4062391BCEE6E10198DE78808> /LZWDecode filter
100 string readstring pop =
<801D0D065101C4EA69319AC4062391BCEE6E10198DE78808>
<< /EarlyChange 0 >> /LZWDecode filter
100 string readstring pop (the quick brown fox) eq =
